---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { publications } from '../../data/showcase';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const sorted = [...publications].sort((a,b) => {
  const ar = a.editions.find(e => e.id === a.currentEdition);
  const br = b.editions.find(e => e.id === b.currentEdition);
  return (br?.releasedAt || '').localeCompare(ar?.releasedAt || '');
});
---
<BaseLayout title="Publications" description="Writing and technical publications I have intentionally released, with edition history and original references.">
  <header class="page-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">WRITING & PUBLISHED WORK</p>
    <h1>Publications</h1>
    <p class="lead">Writing helps me discover what I understand, where my explanations are incomplete, and how an idea can become useful to somebody else.</p>
  </header>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="writing-why">
    <div class="body-copy narrative-copy">
      <h2 id="writing-why">I write to make difficult ideas navigable.</h2>
      <p>There are subjects I can discuss comfortably but cannot explain cleanly until I try to write them down. Writing demands more than recognizing an idea. It asks me to choose definitions, establish boundaries, connect claims to evidence, and build a path that another reader can follow.</p>
      <p>Much of my interest lies in technical and educational writing: foundational explanations, frameworks, articles, manuals, and long-form treatments of complex systems. I want a reader to understand why the parts fit together, not merely receive a catalog of terms. I also want the language to be precise enough that unanswered questions remain visible.</p>
      <p>Not every manuscript is ready for publication. I separate the process of developing an idea from the decision to release it. This section is reserved for work that I've intentionally made public, with its edition and available reference materials clearly identified.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="published-works">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">RELEASED EDITIONS</p><h2 id="published-works">Writing ready to be read.</h2></div>
      <p>When a work is released, it should be possible to identify which edition a reader is seeing and where the original can be found.</p>
    </div>
    {sorted.length > 0 ? (
      <div class="card-grid">
        {sorted.map(pub => {
          const edition = pub.editions.find(e => e.id === pub.currentEdition);
          return (
            <article class="card">
              <p class="status">{pub.kind} · {edition?.releasedAt}</p>
              <h3><a href={base + '/publications/' + pub.slug + '/'}>{pub.title}</a></h3>
              <p>{pub.summary}</p>
              <p class="knowledge-status">Current published edition: {edition?.version}</p>
              <a class="text-link" href={base + '/publications/' + pub.slug + '/'}>Read the publication ↗</a>
            </article>
          );
        })}
      </div>
    ) : (
      <div class="curation-empty" role="status">
        <h3>The publication collection is being curated.</h3>
        <p>I haven't selected a released work for this collection yet. My <a href={base + '/knowledge/'}>Knowledge library</a> is available now, including substantive references and transparently labeled drafts and development materials. I keep that evolving reference collection distinct from formal publications.</p>
      </div>
    )}
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="narrative-panel">
      <h2>What makes a publication useful over time?</h2>
      <p>Good technical writing can change as the evidence changes. When I publish a revised edition, I want the release history to make that evolution visible rather than silently replacing the past. Where available, a publication page will provide native reading, downloadable formats, an original source, and a suggested citation.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href={base + '/about/interests/'}>Read why writing matters to me ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
